          "question": "Got stung by a wasp an hour ago, my hand has ballooned and now my eyelids look puffy and I feel strange.",
          "answer": "**Call emergency services now (999 in the UK, 911 in the US, or your local number). Do not wait to see if it gets better.**\n\nA wasp sting followed by spreading swelling and a feeling of being unwell can be a sign of a severe allergic reaction. The retrieved guidance on anaphylaxis (Allergy and emergency medicine society guidance) says this can be fatal and can develop within minutes to a couple of hours of a sting. It lists swelling of the lips, tongue or throat, breathlessness, dizziness or faintness, and a weak, rapid pulse as warning signs. Your puffy eyelids and feeling strange are the kind of change that needs checking urgently.\n\nThe safety tool returned a \"routine\" rating. That rating does not rule out an emergency. The tool only checks a fixed list of patterns, and this guidance matches your situation closely enough that you should treat it as urgent.\n\nWhile you wait for help:\n- Tell the call handler about the sting and your symptoms.\n- Do not drive yourself. Do not walk off alone.\n- If you feel faint, lie down and raise your legs slightly.\n- If you have been prescribed an emergency allergy medicine, use it exactly as your clinician told you.\n- If your breathing or throat changes, or you collapse, tell the responders straight away.\n\nThe guidance I could retrieve does not cover what to do about hand swelling on its own, or how to manage a sting at home. Please get in-person medical care for this rather than relying on this tool.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
